Hello All3DP team,

a pitch with a five-minute test at the end of it.

Open any downloaded STL that carries a honeycomb pattern, a knurl or a set of
ribs in a mesh tool, and you get hundreds of individual faces. There is
nothing useful to edit — the pattern was never stored as a structure, only as
geometry.

Solidon3D 0.5.0 recognises it as one feature: a pattern with pitch, cell width
and depth, a single entry in the object tree. One click removes it; three
numbers set it again, and the cells stay where they were. This also works
around a handle: a texture on a cylinder follows the curvature, so the grooves
are equally deep everywhere and the pattern closes around the circumference
without a seam — the pitch moves to the value that divides evenly, and Solidon
says which one.

Underneath sits the larger change of this release: the mesh becomes an exact
body. Primitives are now built with real faces and edges, and holes, slots,
countersinks, bosses, beads, grooves and threads stay exact when you move,
duplicate, rotate or remove them. STEP goes in and comes back out cleanly. The
four options that used to switch between mesh and exact kernels are gone —
there is no kernel choice in the interface any more.

Also in this release:

* Dimensions appear on the model itself: click a hole and type its distances to
  the edges, its centre and its diameter, with a live preview.
* An existing thread gets its counterpart on the other part at the press of a
  button, in a table size, and as a fit.
* Resin printers are supported, with pixel size and minimum wall in the
  profile.
* A model with open areas is closed while it is read instead of merely being
  reported.
* Speed: a 200,000-triangle plate is recognised in one second; moving or
  rotating it answers in half a second instead of eight.

That is 70 user-visible changes in this release; 896 since the first 0.1 demo.

If you want to try it: download any model with a knurl, click the pattern in
the object tree, and set a different pitch. That is the one-minute version of
this story.

Solidon3D is a desktop application for Windows, macOS and Linux. One-time
purchase, no subscription, no account: after a single activation it runs fully
offline. Interface and manual are available in six languages, and there is a
demo.

